We compressed by arm, cohort and segment, which gives 16 records for 91,004 rows. Every statistic is a sum, so merging cells is exact: summing the records of the casual and power cells of a cohort gives the record we would have computed by compressing on cohort alone. We compress once at the finest keys we might need, and merge later.

From the record to any estimate
From the record of cell \(k\) we get the cell mean and the covariance of that mean,
\[\bar u_k = \frac{1}{G_k} \sum_{c \in k} u_c, \qquad \widehat{\text{Cov}}(\bar u_k) = \frac{1}{G_k}\left(\frac{1}{G_k} \sum_{c \in k} u_c u_c^\top - \bar u_k \bar u_k^\top\right).\]
The second formula is the heteroskedasticity robust covariance of a mean, written in terms of the stored sums. Members are independent (A1) and the cells do not overlap, so the cell means are independent of each other. The post-stratified arm mean is \(\mu_z = \sum_h w_h \bar u_{z,h}\), where \(w_h\) is the share of all members in stratum \(h\), and every estimate is a function \(g\) of the stacked cell means \(\theta = (\bar u_1, \bar u_2, \dots)\):







Estimate
\(g\)




ITT on hours per row
\(\mu_1^Y / \mu_1^n - \mu_0^Y / \mu_0^n\)


LATE on hours per row
\(\left(\mu_1^Y / \mu_1^n - \mu_0^Y / \mu_0^n\right) / \left(\mu_1^{nT} / \mu_1^n - \mu_0^{nT} / \mu_0^n\right)\)


ITT on rebuffers per play
\(\mu_1^N / \mu_1^D - \mu_0^N / \mu_0^D\)



The delta method gives \(\widehat{\text{Var}}(g(\hat\theta)) = \nabla g^\top \, \widehat{\text{Cov}}(\hat\theta) \, \nabla g\), where \(\widehat{\text{Cov}}(\hat\theta)\) is block diagonal with one block per cell. When \(g\) is linear, \(\nabla g\) is exactly a delta vector: \(+w_h\) on the outcome component of each treated cell and \(-w_h\) on each control cell. For a nonlinear \(g\), the gradient plays the same role, so \(\nabla g\) is the delta vector generalized to any smooth effect.
We compute \(\nabla g\) with complex step differentiation. It adds an imaginary step \(i\kappa\) to one component \(\theta_l\) at a time and reads the derivative from the imaginary part, \(\partial g / \partial \theta_l = \text{Im}\, g(\theta + i\kappa \mathbb{1}_l) / \kappa\), where \(\mathbb{1}_l\) is 1 in component \(l\) and 0 elsewhere. There is no subtraction, so a step of \(\kappa = 10^{-20}\) gives the derivative to machine precision for any \(g\) built from arithmetic.


